.TH CLOG_CLOSE 3 "2026-07-10" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
clog_close \- flush, close, and free a logger
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/clogger.h>

void clog_close(clog logger);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Releases every resource that the logger owns:
  - For a logger on a file, the call closes the file descriptor when
    this is the last handle that shares the file.  While another
    handle (the root or a derived logger) is open, the fd stays open
    and the call only lowers the refcount of the file.  A handle that
    a second clog_create_from_file_mp(3) of the same rotating file
    returns shares the file the same way.
  - With async logging on (see clog_create_from_fd_mp(3)) and this the
    last handle, the call drains everything that is already queued and
    joins the writer thread before it releases the output (and, for a
    logger on a file, closes the file descriptor).  That drain is the
    last delivery attempt of the logger and nothing is retried after
    it, so whatever it cannot deliver is replaced by a "log record
    truncated: undelivered batch dropped (records=<n> bytes=<m>): the
    logger closed" record.
    The one exception is a logger whose drain at exit(3), or at a
    CLOG_FATAL call, gave up because its writer thread did not answer
    in time.  Closing such a logger, from a destructor for example,
    neither waits for that thread nor joins it, and the logger is left
    in place for the end of the process.
  - When the logger compresses its rotated files (see
    clog_create_from_file_mp(3)) and this is the last handle, the call
    waits until every compression that a rotation queued has finished
    and then stops the compression thread.  So by the time the call
    returns, every rotated file of the logger is compressed, or left
    whole and uncompressed after a failed compression.
  - It frees every key=value field of the logger.
  - It frees the internal write buffer.
  - It frees the state of this handle, and frees the shared backing
    store only when it releases the last handle.

Do not use the handle after this call.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
logger  Logger handle to close.
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
Do not call clog_close() while another thread makes any call with the
same handle: a clog_<level> macro, clog_set_level, clog_set_field and
the functions next to it, clog_derive, or another clog_close().  You
must make sure that every other use of a handle has stopped before you
close it.  This rule does not cover a sibling handle that keeps logging
while one handle closes (the root against a derived logger, or two
derived loggers); that close is safe, because the sibling is a
different handle and it is open.
For a logger on an fd, from clog_create_from_fd_mp, this call does NOT
close the file descriptor; you have to do that yourself.
Asserts when logger is CLOG_NULL, stale or already closed.  The
destructor of the library releases the table of loggers at the end of
the process, so a close from a destructor that runs after it finds
every handle stale and reports the same error.
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR clog_create_from_fd_mp (3),
.BR clog_create_from_file_mp (3)
